Z||000000||fsrowsell||01-21-2001||07:20 PM||||Hi Guys & Gals, I just purchased this 86SE 2.8L. It is in good shape, but the only thing wrong is it won't start. I checked the fuel and thats OK. there is fuel going to the injector rails. Thats all I have done so far. I thought before I started to tear the thing appart I would ask help from the experts here on the form. Anyone have any suggestions or ideas on what the problem could be? Thanks Felix<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fieroboy1||01-21-2001||07:54 PM||||check for spark. Should you find none the problem is most likely either the module which is under the distributor cap, the coil which is mounted to the engine in the vicinity of the distributor or it's the pickup coil which is on the distributor.  If there is no spark and the distributor is looking corroded and generally unhealthy i suggest checking the coil for resistance with an ohmeter and if that passes then put in a rebuilt distributor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fsrowsell||01-21-2001||08:09 PM||||<BR>Thanks, I just check for spark on the spark plugs and there was spark. So now I will take a look in side the distributor and see what it looks like.... Felix<P>||||reg||5
Z||000003||ka4nkf||01-21-2001||11:06 PM||||One thing i always check is the check engine light. turn the switch key on and the check engine light should come on. if it dosent then the computer has lost power. there is a pigtail in the wiring harness near the battery. check and make sure it is connected.it won't run if it is disconnected.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fsrowsell||01-21-2001||11:47 PM||||I took the Distributor cap off and what a mess inside. Allot of corrision inside on the poles. I took the distributor out and will clear it up tomorrow. Too late now, Even with the distributor out, it was hard to turn the shaft. Because of the corroded poles... I guest we will see tomorrow when I clean it up and put it back together. If it was my problem or not.<P>KA4NKF... I did check the pig tail and it is pluged in.. Thanks<BR>Felix<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||martin1875||01-22-2001||12:43 AM||||if i were you i would buy new plugs and wires ,cap and rotor. any time i buy a vehicle its one of the first things i do. who knows when it was last done.if the distributer does't turn freely i would change it to. i also change the oil and filter every time i get a new car or a used car new to me.if that dosent help check the timing, be sure you install distributer in the right fireing order. i bought a 86se a couple of months ago changed the plugs and wires and it would't start.someone before me installed it off buy a couple teeth, had to move the wires on the cap around to match the the fireing order. let us know what works.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fsrowsell||01-23-2001||12:25 AM||||Will no Joy... I cleaned up the poles and the rest, check the coil in the Dis. and it was OK, put it back on and no deference. Still won't start. The plugs, wires, Dis cap and rotor button all look new.<BR>Is there any way of checking the Module, without having to buy a new one. Also were is Number one on the Dis. Cap? I got the firing order but I don't know were number one is, so I can check to see if the wires are on in the right order.<BR>Thanks Felix<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fieroboy1||01-23-2001||12:04 PM||||You did post that you have spark though correct?  Are you getting spark at the plugs? If this is the case then you shouldn't be looking at the module.  If you don't already have a Haynes or chilton manual get one.  That will show you which cylinder is number one and what the firing order is. It doesnt matter where on the distributor you start so long as the wires go in order and they go to the correct cylinder.  If you didn't put the distributor back in in the correct orientation you can screw up the timing. So make sure it's oriented the same way.  If you have spark at the plugs than spark isn't you're problem unless it looks really yellowy and weak.  But like we posted earlier it is better just to get a rebuilt distributor if the old one looks shot. This will have a new module and pick up coil already installed.  Perform a compression test on the engine and see if all the cylinders are pretty close in value.  Compression gauges are fairly cheap and are a great diagnostic tool to let you know what is going on in the engine.<p>[This message has been edited by Fieroboy1 (edited 01-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||fsrowsell||01-23-2001||09:11 PM||||Fieroboy1, yes I do have spark at the plugs... I also have fuel going to the MPFI. I took the gas line off were it connects to the Schrader Valve and turn on the pump, to see if there was fuel. but I don't know if the injectors are being supplied with fuel or the cylinder. Is there a way to test the injectors for fuel delivery?<BR>I also noticed that there was no air filter, so I bought one. I don't know what damage that would off caused. I will also check my compresion has will, to night.<BR>Felix<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fsrowsell||02-03-2001||01:56 PM||||Will, I'm still trying to solve the problem with the starting. This is what I have done so far..... I replaced the Module,Pickup Coil, Rotor button and cap.I place number one on TDC, to check my timing and it was correct. I also ckecked the compression and they were all within 8psi. But one thing I did notice while doing the compression test was my plugs were wet. So, she must be getting fuel. and I do have spark at the plugs. After doing all this I turn the engine over to start it again and engine sounded like she was flooding and you could smell the fuel comeing out of the exchause pipes. Any more ideas on things I should check. My fuel pressure regulator gone or what.....<BR>Thanks<BR> <P>[||||reg||1
Z||000010||turbo86se||02-03-2001||06:25 PM||||I'm probably wrong, and way off, but I had starting problems with my automatic 86SE V6. I had to replace the neutral safety switch. Cleaned up the battery terminals too, which to this day still get loose.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||02-03-2001||07:02 PM||||Is more than one plug sparking. I'm thinking here, and you've got at least 2 of the three things needed for an engine to run, and I see no reason why air would be the problem. My only idea is you're not getting spark at the appropriate time. Is the cam turning?||||reg||1
Z||000012||fsrowsell||02-03-2001||10:16 PM||||turbo86se, did your engine turn over and wouldn't start or did it just do nothing. Like it had no power going to the starter. Mine is also an automatic and she turns over ok.<BR>The cam I guest must be turning because my Rotor button is turning in my Dis.<P>I desided to dig a little fearther, so I took off the Plenum. First thing I noticed was the trottle body was really durty. Next thing was when I went to disconect the EGR tube from the  Plenum. The tube was not conected to the Plenum and then when I took the Plenum off. I look under neath where it was supose to have been bolted, I found that it was blocked off with file paper and was not conected to the tube at all. Will this prevent the engine from starting?? I'll keep everyone posted as I go.... Thanks<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||02-03-2001||11:58 PM||||keep hunting erg tube if blocked off and not cause of vac leak is ok not the trouble. erg is cause of dirty tb + manfold before it was blocked off and is normal. wet or old bad gas? use gas condisioner. try starting fluid?<BR>ch fuel pressure , pumps go out but still pump just not enuff pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fsrowsell||02-04-2001||11:33 PM||||Today I worked on cleaning up parts. I cleaned the tb, intake manfold, Fuel rail and injectors. The ports on the intake were a mess. The fuel rail and injectors looked very clean. Can one apply power to an injector to make it spray? I will start puting it back together tomorrow after work. I have to get some new gaskets. With reference to the gas. I don't know how old the gas is. The gas hand shows a full tank. Is there a way to drain the tank? Or will the condisioner restore the gas. Starting fluid, can I just spray it into the trottle body? I checked my Fuel pressure and it was OK. Thanks<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>keep hunting erg tube if blocked off and not cause of vac leak is ok not the trouble. erg is cause of dirty tb + manfold before it was blocked off and is normal. wet or old bad gas? use gas condisioner. try starting fluid?<BR>ch fuel pressure , pumps go out but still pump just not enuff pressure.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||fsrowsell||02-11-2001||11:00 PM||||Will I'm still at it and I think I'm getting closer. I cleaned every thing up and put new parts on. New plugs etc. Then the time came for the truth. Was all that work worth it or not.<BR>So, I turned the key and she started to turn...then faster, but would stop when I let the key go, she would stop. It was like she was flooding herself. I had a strong smill of gas and when I started pulling the plugs. They were really wet with gas. So, I know she is getting spark and maybe now its that she is getting too much gas. So, whats my next step. Is it the regulator or what? Any body with any ideas. The weather is getting really nice and would love to go around the block at lease once.... Thanks<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Indiana_resto_guy||02-11-2001||11:16 PM||||Are you absoultly sure that you have #1 up to TDC? It sort of sounds like you may be 180 degrees out. Use the WIDEST mark on the crankshaft balancer (there are 3 marks) hand crank the engine to this mark (disconnect the battery) and see where the rotor sits, if you have a compression gauge install it to #1 before you turn it by hand. When it builds some pressure comming around to the widest mark, you'll know #1 is up. Adjust if necessary, this should be it as all other combinations for start are present.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fsrowsell||02-12-2001||01:09 AM||||Correct me if I'm wrong. #1 is first one on the right, facing the engine and on the trunk side right? ie 6-4-2<BR>                     5-3-1<BR>                   Trunk side<BR>                facing the engine <BR>I have a compression tester, so I will check it tomorrow. Thanks I will let you know what happens<BR>              <P><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||fsrowsell||02-12-2001||09:25 PM||||Indiana_resto_guy, I did has you said. I placed her on tdc and I aligned the rotor button with number one wire. Then I turned her over acouple of time and them recheck it by putting her back at TDC. To make sure the timeing chain wasn't sliping or enything. But she was good. I then rechecked my spark on all clyiners and I had fire on all six. even though number one looked a little weak. So, if it bad gas. How do I get read of it, with out droping the tank. I have a full tank of gas. I know she is getting lots of gas. Because every time I take my plugs out, they are very wet. Anyone with any more ideas...Thanks.... I'm at a lost....<BR>It just doesn't make sence... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Indiana_resto_guy||02-12-2001||10:03 PM||||OK, as I understand the post you bought this car NOT running, correct? You have since replaced the Module, Pickup Coil, Rotor button and cap (new or known good parts?).<BR>If all good or new check that service engine light 'lights' up, the EGR valve isn't stuck open, the TPS sensor isn't stuck wide open. Check the cold start valve for proper operation and the MAF sensor (check that first) simple, just disconnect it.<BR>Let us know what you come up with.||||reg||1
Z||000020||stsmithgt||02-13-2001||05:31 PM||||I didn't get it.. You did change the coil, the one that goes to the middle of the distributor cap.?  My impression was that you change the pick-up coil and module, but not the main coil that spits out the spark to the distributor.  I've had parts where they would check out fine with a meter, but try to put them to work and the part fails.  I hate it when that happens.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Kind of like the cars  battery reading 12 volts, but has no power behind it to function properly.<P>||||reg||1
